<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Montesquieu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1689">1689</a>–<a href="/years/1755">1755</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Charles-Louis de Secondat, Baron de Montesquieu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Montesquieu, Charles-Louis de Secondat, Baron de La Brède et de Montesquieu, was a French magistrate and political philosopher of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. His Persian Letters of 1721 satirised French society, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-spirit-of-the-laws">The Spirit of the Laws</a> of 1748 compared forms of government and argued for the separation of legislative, executive and judicial powers. His ideas strongly influenced the framers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montesquieu.jpg" alt="Montesquieu" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Jacques-Antoine Dassier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Montesquieu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Enlightenment of <b>Montesquieu</b>, Voltaire, Rousseau and Diderot's Encyclopédie was largely conducted in French.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its constitution, praised by <b>Montesquieu</b> in The Spirit of the Laws (1748), influenced European reformers and the framers of the United States Constitution.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Polybius explained Rome’s success by its mixed constitution of monarchic, aristocratic and democratic elements, an idea taken up by Machiavelli, <b>Montesquieu</b> and the framers of the United States Constitution, who borrowed the Senate, the veto and Roman pseudonyms such as Publius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Enlightenment writers such as <b>Montesquieu</b> and Adam Smith attacked slavery as unjust or wasteful, and the evangelical leader John Wesley condemned it in his Thoughts upon Slavery of 1774.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Locke grounded government in natural rights to life, liberty and property and in the consent of the governed; <b>Montesquieu</b> urged the separation of legislative, executive and judicial power; and Jean-Jacques Rousseau, whose The Social Contract opens “Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains”, placed sovereignty…</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>…servant of the state”, restricted torture and began the codification of Prussian law; Catherine the Great drew on <b>Montesquieu</b> in her Instruction of 1767; and Joseph II, sole ruler of the Habsburg lands from 1780 to 1790, granted religious toleration and abolished personal serfdom in the Bohemian lands…</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Château de la Brède, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.68,-0.54&amp;year=1731" aria-label="Château de la Brède on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4537 584 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Montesquieu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4987 854h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4987 854h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.68,-0.54&amp;year=1731">What was here in 1731? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…constant experience shews us, that every man invested with power is apt to abuse it; he pushes on till he comes to the utmost limit. Is it not strange, though true, to say, that virtue itself has need of limits?&#8221;</p><footer>The Spirit of Laws, book 11, chapter 4 &#183; tr. Thomas Nugent (1758 edition) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Spirit_of_Laws_(1758)/Book_XI" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When the legislative and executive powers are united in the same person, or in the same body of magistrates, there can be no liberty…&#8221;</p><footer>The Spirit of Laws, book 11, chapter 6 &#183; tr. Thomas Nugent (1758 edition) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Spirit_of_Laws_(1758)/Book_XI" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It has been very well said, that if triangles were to make to themselves gods, they would give them three sides.&#8221;</p><footer>Persian Letters, letter 59 &#183; tr. John Davidson (1899 edition) &#183; from a letter of Rica, one of the novel's Persian travellers &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Persian_Letters/Letter_59" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Liberty is a right of doing whatever the laws permit; and if a citizen could do what they forbid, he would no longer be possest of liberty, because all his fellow citizens would have the same power.&#8221;</p><footer>The Spirit of Laws, book 11, chapter 3 &#183; tr. Thomas Nugent (1758 edition) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Spirit_of_Laws_(1758)/Book_XI" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Oh! ah! A Persian, is he? Most amazing! However can anybody be a Persian?&#8221;</p><footer>Persian Letters, letter 30 &#183; tr. John Davidson (1899 edition) &#183; the Parisians' wonder at the visitor Rica, who tells it in the letter &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Persian_Letters/Letter_30" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
